WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.580
Top secret binary code messages, revolutionizing&nbsp;
the world of mathematics, and the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:10.620
a well-placed burp: these are just a few&nbsp;
hidden treats in the odyssey of Futurama.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:16.140
"Futurama" was pretty much the perfect title&nbsp;
for an ambitious show about the amazing world of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.314
tomorrow. And yet, creator Matt Groening had some&nbsp;
other names in mind that he originally liked more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.314 --> 00:00:25.740
On the first season DVD director's commentary,&nbsp;
he revealed that his first idea for the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:31.602
was "Aloha, Mars." His other proposed option was&nbsp;
the significantly more pessimistic "Doomsville."

00:00:31.602 --> 00:00:34.860
But it sounds like nobody else working on&nbsp;
the show was particularly psyched about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:40.620
those possibilities. The title they eventually&nbsp;
settled on was lifted from the 1939 to 1940 New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:45.540
York World's Fair. As visitors were welcomed&nbsp;
into "the world of tomorrow!," many of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:50.580
lined up for the General Motors Pavilion and&nbsp;
its exhibit called "Futurama," which offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.260
a glimpse into the far-off futuristic&nbsp;
year of 1960. Appropriately enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.999
on the show's pilot episode, a cryogenic facility&nbsp;
employee references that exhibit's slogan.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.980
"Welcome to the world of tomorrow!"

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.020
"Why do you always have to say it that way?"

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.540
On The Simpsons, Matt Groening's first show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.260
several of the main characters were named&nbsp;
after Groening's immediate family. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.700
Homer and Marge's namesakes are Groening's&nbsp;
parents. Futurama's lead character Philip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.469
J. Fry was also named after Groening's&nbsp;
father, as Philip was his middle name.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:26.400
When production began on Futurama in 1998,&nbsp;
two of the most important roles were locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.480
down before voice recording began. For&nbsp;
Fry, producers picked Charlie Schlatter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.960
a veteran actor who was best known for&nbsp;
playing the title role in the short-lived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.640
TV adaptation of Ferris Bueller. As for&nbsp;
Leela, Nicole Sullivan got the job. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.069
the time, she was a breakout star on the&nbsp;
late-night Fox sketch comedy show Mad TV.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:47.460
But then suddenly producers changed their&nbsp;
minds and decided to replace both of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.120
actors. Billy West, who was best known at&nbsp;
the time for his work on The Howard Stern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.280
Show and Ren &amp; Stimpy, stepped in as Fry.&nbsp;
And taking over as Leela was Katey Sagal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.708
who was best known as matriarch Peg Bundy on&nbsp;
Fox's long-running sitcom Married...with Children.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:07.200
Bender Bending Rodriguez, the show's&nbsp;
resident sociopathic, alcoholic robot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.720
is perhaps Futurama's most beloved character.&nbsp;
A big part of that is thanks to John DiMaggio's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.860
excellent voice work. The actor discussed his&nbsp;
inspiration during an appearance for his signature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:21.900
character on The Kevin Pollak Chat Show. He took&nbsp;
a little bit from cowboy movie actor Slim Pickens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.509
who's probably best known among comedy fans&nbsp;
for Blazing Saddles and Dr. Strangelove.

00:02:26.509 --> 00:02:31.500
DiMaggio was also inspired by drunk barflies and a&nbsp;
little bit from a character that a college friend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.300
once performed called "Charlie the Sausage&nbsp;
Lover." DiMaggio initially auditioned for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.800
role of Professor Farnsworth with his unique&nbsp;
raspy blend of surliness, gregariousness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.256
and self-importance. He didn't get that role, but&nbsp;
he managed to book Bender using the same voice.

00:02:46.256 --> 00:02:48.462
"Glad I'm not him, whoever he is."

00:02:48.462 --> 00:02:53.400
Dr. John Zoidberg is a wholly original&nbsp;
creation. A man of contradictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.800
he's lobster-esque but also human-like. He's a&nbsp;
doctor, and yet also somehow poor. Best of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.060
he's a creature of the 31st century from&nbsp;
another planet who inexplicably talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.620
like an old Vaudeville comedian. Billy West&nbsp;
pulled from two major old-timey inspirations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:13.020
when putting together Zoidberg's voice: actors&nbsp;
George Jessel and Lou Jacobi. That background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.520
definitely provides a Jewish Yiddish flavor to&nbsp;
the character, which West thought was appropriate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.342
considering the traditionally Jewish&nbsp;
"-berg" suffix in his last name.

00:03:21.342 --> 00:03:25.920
The "Zoid" part of the name, meanwhile, is a&nbsp;
futuristic, alien-sounding word that has personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.320
significance for Futurama producer and writer&nbsp;
David X. Cohen. It comes from a computer game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.660
named "Zoid," which Cohen created when he was just&nbsp;
a kid. He submitted it to Broderbund Software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.200
but they ended up rejecting it. They also&nbsp;
spelled his name wrong in the rejection letter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.456
which sounds like something that could've&nbsp;
happened to Zoidberg on an episode of Futurama.

00:03:44.456 --> 00:03:49.560
The late, great Phil Hartman specialized in&nbsp;
playing characters both obnoxious and oblivious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.500
such as Bill McNeal on NewsRadio. He also voiced&nbsp;
the sleazy lawyer Lionel Hutz and the washed-up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.520
actor Troy McClure on The Simpsons. So&nbsp;
it makes perfect sense that he was cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.620
to provide the voice of the obnoxious, Captain&nbsp;
Kirk-esque Captain Zapp Brannigan on Futurama.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:10.920
But then in 1998, tragedy stuck when Hartman was&nbsp;
murdered by his wife Brynn. After his passing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.940
Billy West stepped into the role. He once had&nbsp;
a conversation with Hartman about their shared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.260
love of old time radio announcers who had&nbsp;
big, booming voices and an inflated sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:24.554
of self-importance. So West ended up playing&nbsp;
Zapp in that style as a tribute to Hartman.

00:04:24.554 --> 00:04:27.494
"You disgust me! Go on."

00:04:27.494 --> 00:04:30.120
Futurama producers really wanted the Beastie Boys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.960
one of the most popular rap groups of&nbsp;
the 20th century, to play themselves in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.580
the 31st century. They made it on, but it&nbsp;
didn't work out exactly as they'd hoped.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:42.660
The plan was to have them appear the same&nbsp;
way that many contemporary celebrities guest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:47.580
starred on Futurama: as disembodied heads,&nbsp;
kept alive in jars over the centuries. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.500
according to the DVD commentary for the episode&nbsp;
"Hell is Other Robots," when writer-producers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.160
Ken Keeler and David X. Cohen went to&nbsp;
New York to meet with the Beasties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.738
they never managed to connect. Eventually, the&nbsp;
band members recorded their vocals separately.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.738 --> 00:05:06.120
Well, Adam "Ad-Rock" Horovitz and Mike Diamond&nbsp;
recorded their tracks without any fuss. But Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:11.022
"MCA" Yauch had a scheduling conflict,&nbsp;
so Ad-Rock just did his lines for him.

00:05:11.022 --> 00:05:14.880
Even after all that, the group wasn't&nbsp;
entirely happy with the material that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:18.720
the Futurama writers had prepared. In the&nbsp;
finished episode, the Beasties perform a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.160
portion of their hit song "Sabotage" a cappella.&nbsp;
They were initially asked to perform "(You Gotta)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.580
Fight for Your Right (To Party)" in this&nbsp;
style, but they refused. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:29.760
there were apparently no hard&nbsp;
feelings lingering afterward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.456
as the group later gave a bit of a shoutout to&nbsp;
Futurama on their 2009 single "Too Many Rappers."

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.440
Futurama was primarily a comedy, but&nbsp;
some episodes would occasionally touch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.520
on Fry's sadness and sense of loss&nbsp;
from being stuck in the future. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:49.387
2002 episode "Jurassic Bark" is perhaps&nbsp;
the most notable example in this regard.

00:05:49.387 --> 00:05:53.340
While visiting a museum, Fry discovers&nbsp;
the fossilized remains of his dog Seymour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.600
Professor Farnsworth then tells him that he&nbsp;
could feasibly take some of Seymour's DNA and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.940
create a clone. During the cloning process, Fry&nbsp;
learns that Seymour lived to be 15. He figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:08.026
that after he disappeared in 1999, his pooch&nbsp;
must have lived a long, happy life without him.

00:06:08.026 --> 00:06:11.640
But then the saddest flashback&nbsp;
in the history of TV occurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:15.600
as it's revealed that Seymour actually&nbsp;
didn't move on. Instead, he waited for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.400
Fry outside the pizzeria where he used to work&nbsp;
for more than a decade. The episode ends with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.094
Seymour closing his eyes, passing away,&nbsp;
and every viewer sobbing uncontrollably.

00:06:25.094 --> 00:06:29.640
Somehow, this episode could've been even&nbsp;
sadder. As revealed on the DVD commentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.180
the writers toyed around with the idea&nbsp;
of Fry finding the remains of his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.747
and then considering, but ultimately&nbsp;
dismissing, the possibility of cloning her.

00:06:37.747 --> 00:06:43.020
"He's just upset 'cause our boy's missing.&nbsp;
Come on, you overgrown rat, lead us to Philip."

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.480
The 2010 Futurama episode "The Prisoner&nbsp;
of Benda" takes the ever-popular body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:53.400
swap trope to absurd new heights. Professor&nbsp;
Farnsworth invents a machine that allows him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.540
and his intern Amy to place each other's&nbsp;
minds into one another's bodies. Then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.660
the rest of the Planet Express crew&nbsp;
gets involved, using the machine to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:05.100
put their brains into each others' bodies&nbsp;
to fulfill various whims and goals. Alas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.099
there's a catch, as the machine can't&nbsp;
be used twice with the same two people.

00:07:09.099 --> 00:07:13.740
In order to get everybody's brains back to their&nbsp;
original bodies, the Harlem Globetrotters come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:18.240
to the rescue with a mathematical theorem.&nbsp;
In the Futurama universe, the Globetrotters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.680
are not only amazing basketball players but&nbsp;
also brilliant scientists and mathematicians.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.160
Another brilliant mathematician is&nbsp;
the episode's writer Ken Keeler,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:30.540
who holds a doctorate in math from Harvard. To&nbsp;
find a mathematically accurate way to switch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:34.320
everybody's brain back into the correct&nbsp;
body, Keeler had to devise a brand-new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.880
algorithm-based mathematical theorem. We&nbsp;
won't overwhelm you with the specifics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.913
but you can trust that Keeler put in the work&nbsp;
to prove the viability of this complicated plot.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.060
Over the years, Billy West has&nbsp;
consistently proven himself to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:51.840
one of the most prolific voice actors&nbsp;
of his generation. On Futurama alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.000
he's voiced more than 40 different characters,&nbsp;
including Fry, Professor Farnsworth, Dr. Zoidberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:01.260
Zapp Brannigan, and dozens of others.&nbsp;
Because of this, he often plays every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:05.831
character in a given scene, requiring him&nbsp;
to act opposite himself multiple times over.

00:08:05.831 --> 00:08:09.120
It would be difficult enough to record&nbsp;
each voice separately while hearing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.420
playback of his other performances.&nbsp;
But West apparently tends to bounce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:16.620
back and forth between the various voices&nbsp;
in real time to play all of the characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.000
in a single recording session. One of his&nbsp;
co-stars, Phil LaMarr, was amazed when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:25.733
saw West acting opposite himself in this way&nbsp;
while recording the show's very first episode.

00:08:25.733 --> 00:08:29.880
"So the entire scene was Billy.&nbsp;
And we all just sat in the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:34.479
and he did the entire thing in&nbsp;
one take, three different voices."

00:08:34.479 --> 00:08:40.260
With the success of Breaking Bad and Better Call&nbsp;
Saul, Bob Odenkirk has enjoyed a meteoric rise to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:44.820
become one of the hottest dramatic actors in the&nbsp;
industry. But before his pivot to prestige TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:49.980
he was beloved in comedy circles particularly&nbsp;
for the sketch series Mr. Show, as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:54.420
his stints on other sketch shows like Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live and The Ben Stiller Show. And he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.020
not the only member of his family known for&nbsp;
making people laugh, as his younger brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:02.760
Bill worked alongside him as a producer on Mr.&nbsp;
Show before joining the Matt Groening stable.

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:07.560
Bill Odenkirk has worked on The Simpsons,&nbsp;
Futurama, and Disenchantment as both a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:12.480
writer and a producer. He joined The Simpsons as&nbsp;
a supervising producer for the show's 15th season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.800
while he was involved with Futurama as&nbsp;
early as Season 2. This has afforded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.680
the Odenkirk brothers even more&nbsp;
opportunities to collaborate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.760
as Bob voiced a character named Chaz&nbsp;
in the Futurama episode "The Why of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.246
Fry," and he played a mob lawyer in the&nbsp;
Simpsons episode "The Fat Blue Line."

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:33.060
On Futurama, there are two distinct alien&nbsp;
languages that can be spotted on screen at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.060 --> 00:09:36.480
various times. At first glance, the&nbsp;
symbols may appear to be gibberish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.040
but they actually form a language that can&nbsp;
be decoded via a substitution cipher into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.120
readable English. The genesis of these&nbsp;
alien languages extends back to a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.255
before Futurama even existed. As David&nbsp;
X. Cohen explained to Wired in 2013,

00:09:50.255 --> 00:09:54.420
"We realized at first in The Simpsons, and&nbsp;
later carrying on the tradition in Futurama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.420 --> 00:09:58.080
that we could hide these jokes in the&nbsp;
background that were called freeze-frame&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:02.594
jokes [...] We tried to cash in on every bit&nbsp;
of real estate in space and time in the show."

00:10:02.594 --> 00:10:06.840
Fans were able to decode the first alien&nbsp;
language almost instantly. The second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.280
version was more complex, and although it was&nbsp;
eventually decoded by some dedicated fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.020
it took more legwork to figure out. Cohen has&nbsp;
revealed that there was a third language ready&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:20.220
to go, but they never ended up implementing&nbsp;
it because it was too complicated and too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.480
time-consuming. The creative team concluded&nbsp;
that it would go uncracked for years and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:29.615
decoding it would require the use of more advanced&nbsp;
computers than what was currently on the market.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:35.220
Futurama is densely packed with jokes on top of&nbsp;
jokes, so it's fitting that co-creator David X.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.780
Cohen's name is also a joke of sorts. As&nbsp;
it turns out, the "X" in his name doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:43.920
stand for anything. Nor is it even his&nbsp;
real middle initial, which is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.840
"S." When he was joining the Writers Guild of&nbsp;
America, there was already a David S. Cohen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.682
and duplicate names aren't allowed in the&nbsp;
guild. As Cohen recalled during a Reddit AMA,

00:10:53.682 --> 00:10:57.360
"I decided that once I was going phony,&nbsp;
I would take the phoniest letter of all."

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:02.280
Cohen also picked "X" because he thought it was&nbsp;
cool and that it sounded the most sci-fi. Back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:06.720
when he was writing for The Simpsons, he wasn't&nbsp;
yet in the Writers Guild and was therefore allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.160
to be credited as David S. Cohen. Then when he&nbsp;
switched his middle initial for Futurama, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:16.253
led to some confusion over whether or not he was&nbsp;
even the same person who worked on The Simpsons.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:21.120
While Futurama certainly has its fair&nbsp;
share of crass and lowbrow comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.620
it also features plenty of elevated humor,&nbsp;
as well as a notable amount of complicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:30.180
math and science. One of the main reasons the&nbsp;
show works as well as it does is because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:34.380
takes its sci-fi concepts seriously. This&nbsp;
approach was inspired by many sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.640
with one of the most obscure being&nbsp;
the late Polish author Stanislaw Lem.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:42.780
Lem was a renowned sci-fi writer who&nbsp;
published novels, short stories, poetry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:47.220
and essays from the 1940s to the 1990s.&nbsp;
He's perhaps best known as the writer of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:51.300
the 1961 novel Solaris, which has&nbsp;
been adapted into multiple movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.820
His work served as a significant influence&nbsp;
on David X. Cohen, who told Wired in 2014,

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:59.340
"My mom was a voracious science fiction reader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:02.460
so actually that's where I got my&nbsp;
love of science fiction, and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:06.360
of the books I found lying around when I&nbsp;
was a kid were the Stanislaw Lem books."

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:10.800
Cohen went on to explain how surreal and funny&nbsp;
the short stories were, and how they did,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.220
"Have a big influence on me,&nbsp;
especially as far as the idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:17.460
that robots could be characters. So&nbsp;
Bender being kind of the most human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:21.300
characters on Futurama I think does&nbsp;
owe a little bit to Stanislaw Lem."

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:26.820
Futurama occasionally ventures into&nbsp;
the anthology format. One of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:31.080
notable examples is "Naturama," which is&nbsp;
presented as a sci-fi nature documentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:34.200
with the main characters appearing&nbsp;
as anthropomorphic animals.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:39.600
The second segment in this episode, "The Pinta&nbsp;
Island Tortoise," refers to a specific species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:44.580
tortoise from the Galápagos Islands. The episode&nbsp;
is dedicated to the memory of Lonesome George,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:49.440
the last living tortoise of his species who&nbsp;
died two years before this half-hour aired.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:53.580
The character of Lonely Hubert is a clear&nbsp;
nod to George, who caught the imagination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:58.920
of the world after he was discovered by a&nbsp;
Hungarian scientist on Pinta Island in 1971.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:03.780
George passed away in 2012 at the Tortoise&nbsp;
Breeding and Rearing Center on Santa Cruz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:07.320
Island. His exact age was unknown,&nbsp;
though scientists estimated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:11.220
he was about a hundred years old. That&nbsp;
might sound like quite the senior animal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:15.889
but giant tortoises of this type can&nbsp;
actually live for twice that long.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:21.960
Leela and Amy are two of the most crucial members&nbsp;
of the Planet Express team. They have a few traits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:26.220
in common, but their core personalities are&nbsp;
drastically different. It may be hard to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:31.080
today, but their personalities were originally&nbsp;
the other way around. Amy was written to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:35.160
tough tomboy type who served as the crew's&nbsp;
mechanic and wore coveralls rather than her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:39.780
trademark pink sweatsuit, while Leela was the&nbsp;
softer of the two in the original pilot script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:44.255
By the time casting was underway, though, it&nbsp;
was clear that a change needed to be made.

00:13:44.255 --> 00:13:47.760
Lauren Tom wound up being cast as&nbsp;
Amy, but there was one big issue:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:51.363
she wasn't a good fit for the&nbsp;
character as she was initially written.

00:13:51.363 --> 00:13:53.894
"That's not really my persona that I put out."

00:13:53.894 --> 00:13:58.200
Luckily, Matt Groening was willing to workshop&nbsp;
the character and retool Amy's personality to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.940
better suit Tom. They were able to keep all&nbsp;
the plans they had for Amy by transferring her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:07.380
rougher attributes to Leela, which ultimately&nbsp;
worked better for everyone. As Tom recalled,

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:11.125
"They made Amy a little bit softer,&nbsp;
kind of free with her love."

00:14:11.125 --> 00:14:13.440
"And then they made Leela the tougher one when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:18.420
Katey Sagal came on. That also fit&nbsp;
perfectly with her, her persona."

00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:21.840
Futurama wouldn't have been the&nbsp;
same without David X. Cohen's input,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.920
but there was a time when it looked like the&nbsp;
show would have to move on without him. Soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:30.180
after production on the show began, Cohen found&nbsp;
himself overwhelmed by the job's heavy workload,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:34.620
the tight schedule, and the high-pressure&nbsp;
demands. The stress reached such a boiling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:38.460
point that he ended up walking away for&nbsp;
a time. As he recalled to Sci-Fi Online,

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:40.800
"A few months into the production of Futurama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:45.540
I got so exhausted that I couldn't do the job&nbsp;
anymore and I actually quit for about 4 days."

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:50.700
Luckily for fans of the show, Cohen was able to&nbsp;
keep his cool and power through all the trials and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:55.920
tribulations. The demanding production schedule&nbsp;
was so extreme that the creators jokingly invented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:01.020
an eighth day of the week called "Blernsday" to be&nbsp;
able to get all of their work done on time. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:05.640
eventually led to the creation of "Blernsball,"&nbsp;
the show's jazzed-up version of baseball.

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:08.767
"Except for the word 'blern,'&nbsp;
that was complete gibberish."

00:15:08.767 --> 00:15:13.620
Bender's apartment is a central location in&nbsp;
Futurama that winds up becoming Fry's home&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:18.360
as well when he moves into the spacious closet,&nbsp;
which is several times larger than the rest of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:23.460
apartment. The apartment building is specifically&nbsp;
designed for robots, and all the door numbers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.460 --> 00:15:30.060
written in binary. Bender's apartment number,&nbsp;
for example, is 00100100. On its own, this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:34.938
simple visual gag, but the joke goes deeper for&nbsp;
anyone willing to put in a little extra effort.

00:15:34.938 --> 00:15:40.020
When the binary is converted into a standard&nbsp;
decimal number, Bender's apartment number is 36,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:45.360
but there's still one more conversion required&nbsp;
to arrive at the final punchline. This last step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.100
requires knowledge of the official ASCII table,&nbsp;
which is the standardized computing chart that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:54.120
breaks down how letters and special characters&nbsp;
are processed by computers. To simplify,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:59.700
binary uses zeros and ones to create all numbers,&nbsp;
and ASCII then uses numbers to create all other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:04.620
characters that are outputted by computers. 36 in&nbsp;
the ASCII table corresponds to the dollar sign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:08.968
which is perfectly appropriate for&nbsp;
Bender's greedy, money-hungry personality.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:15.000
Hermes Conrad is the bureaucrat who serves as the&nbsp;
Planet Express crew's accountant. He notably has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.260
a Jamaican accent, and the show frequently&nbsp;
references his Caribbean heritage. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.260 --> 00:16:24.120
has included entire storylines spotlighting&nbsp;
his journey as a contender in Olympic limbo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.120 --> 00:16:29.629
competitions. But as hard as it may be to imagine&nbsp;
today, Hermes almost wasn't from Jamaica at all.

00:16:29.629 --> 00:16:34.380
When Phil LaMarr first landed the part of&nbsp;
Hermes, he leaned far more heavily into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:38.280
nerdy accountant side of the character. This&nbsp;
approach utilized LaMarr's natural American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:42.300
accent, and he even recorded the first&nbsp;
three episodes of the show like this. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:46.320
it was clear that it wasn't quite firing&nbsp;
on all cylinders. Matt Groening was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:49.966
one who had the idea to switch things&nbsp;
up. As LaMarr recalled to Vanity Fair,

00:16:49.966 --> 00:16:53.506
"Adding the accent gave the character&nbsp;
another dimension, added depth."

00:16:53.506 --> 00:16:56.160
"Because all of a sudden instead&nbsp;
of just writing accountant jokes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.944
they were writing Jamaican jokes."

00:16:58.944 --> 00:17:03.565
LaMarr happily changed his approach, and the&nbsp;
character finally felt right moving forward.

00:17:03.565 --> 00:17:06.325
"Had Matt not suggested that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.325 --> 00:17:09.480
I probably wouldn't be sitting here. I probably&nbsp;
wouldn't have made it past episode four."

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.240
As the voice of Bender and&nbsp;
numerous smaller characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:17.460
John DiMaggio has been an indispensable part of&nbsp;
Futurama since the beginning. And yet somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:22.500
the Hulu reboot version of the show nearly moved&nbsp;
forward without him. The rest of the main cast had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:27.300
already agreed to return, but DiMaggio was holding&nbsp;
out for higher pay. He considered the rates being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:32.280
offered to the cast by Hulu's parent company&nbsp;
Disney to be woefully insufficient. And he wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.280 --> 00:17:36.840
just trying to get his own pay increased but also&nbsp;
those of his co-stars. When news of the dispute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:41.280
broke, fans were up in arms about one of their&nbsp;
favorite voice actors potentially being replaced.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:45.660
DiMaggio revealed that the plan to produce&nbsp;
the show without him was to have Bender's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:49.680
voice change from episode to episode with&nbsp;
different guest stars taking turns with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:53.460
character. Ultimately, though, DiMaggio&nbsp;
ended up returning and the full cast was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.460 --> 00:17:57.900
thus reunited once again, but the situation&nbsp;
wasn't resolved as amicably as possible.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:02.580
DiMaggio's return had many fans thinking he&nbsp;
successfully landed the pay raise that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:07.320
was after, but he actually didn't receive any&nbsp;
additional money in the end. According to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:11.280
Disney was completely unwilling to&nbsp;
negotiate or offer fair compensation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:15.720
so he chose to return to Futurama&nbsp;
simply out of love for the series.

00:18:15.720 --> 00:18:17.160
"I deserve that."

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:22.260
Futurama was co-created by Matt Groening and&nbsp;
David X. Cohen, though the former is sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.260 --> 00:18:26.940
totally lost when the show gets complicated.&nbsp;
Many of the geekier references go over his head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:31.380
and he freely admits that much of the hard&nbsp;
science and mathematics is just gibberish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.380 --> 00:18:35.940
to him. But this is part of why he and&nbsp;
Cohen make such a great team. Cohen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.360
most of the other writers are all experts&nbsp;
on the show's far-ranging subject matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:43.140
particularly producer Ken Keeler,&nbsp;
who has a Ph.D. in applied math.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:47.880
In a 2013 interview with the Los Angeles&nbsp;
Times, Groening admitted that he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:52.740
no idea how space travel in the Futurama&nbsp;
universe actually works. As he explained,

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:56.460
"Originally we planned to have the&nbsp;
spaceship powered by the MW drive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.460 --> 00:19:00.600
which is 'magic wand' — all science&nbsp;
fiction has that magic wand where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:04.800
can travel through galaxies very quickly.&nbsp;
And, instead David had a friend who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.800 --> 00:19:08.460
a theory that you could slow down the speed&nbsp;
of light and therefore make things faster."

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:12.120
Groening went along with it, choosing&nbsp;
to trust his writing staff and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.120 --> 00:19:15.529
knowledge of theoretical space travel&nbsp;
and advanced mathematics, saying,

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:19.860
"I never understood it, but that's what happens.&nbsp;
And the ship is fueled by dark matter, whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:25.556
that is. Again David Cohen could explain,&nbsp;
as could all the writing staff, except me."

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:30.180
There's been a ton of overlap between&nbsp;
The Simpsons and Futurama over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:34.440
which has extended into the shows' comic&nbsp;
series and other related media. But what's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:38.460
perhaps less well-known is the extensive&nbsp;
connective tissue between Futurama and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.720
the animated Netflix show Disenchantment,&nbsp;
which was also co-created by Matt Groening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:47.040
and utilizes David X. Cohen as a writer&nbsp;
and producer. Disenchantment is littered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:50.280
with hidden jokes and Easter eggs,&nbsp;
including many references to Futurama.

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:54.660
Groening co-created Disenchantment with&nbsp;
Josh Weinstein, who also worked on The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:58.860
Simpsons and Futurama as a writer and&nbsp;
producer. Weinstein spoke with Wiretap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:03.720
in 2021 about the Futurama references that&nbsp;
he snuck into Disenchantment and confirmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:07.500
that there are still more connections&nbsp;
left to be discovered. As he divulged,

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:12.360
"Fans started to realize certain things — like&nbsp;
Dreamland is the ancestral home of the Elves. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:16.920
there are other things people have not realized at&nbsp;
all that have to do with the deep, canonical arc."

00:20:16.920 --> 00:20:19.800
Weinstein also dispelled one&nbsp;
particular fan theory that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:23.400
could lead viewers to expect a reveal&nbsp;
that's never coming. As he explained,

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:25.620
"People have this complex&nbsp;
theory that Disenchantment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.620 --> 00:20:28.800
takes place in that future that we&nbsp;
saw with Fry and the time machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:33.791
and that is not true. This is the first time&nbsp;
I'm saying that, because I love fan theories."

00:20:33.791 --> 00:20:37.151
"Bite my shiny metal axe!"

00:20:37.151 --> 00:20:42.060
Amy Wong is known for occasionally burping&nbsp;
quite loudly, but belching on command is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:45.600
something that her voice actor Lauren&nbsp;
Tom has trouble with. Rather than using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:49.440
a stock burp sound effect or faking it&nbsp;
in some other way, the Futurama team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.440 --> 00:20:54.000
has utilized some of Tom's co-stars. John&nbsp;
DiMaggio has filled in as her burp double,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:58.860
as has Maurice LaMarche, who voices several&nbsp;
characters on the show, most notably Kif.

00:20:58.860 --> 00:21:04.740
For LaMarche, burping is no casual exercise. He&nbsp;
takes this bodily function quite seriously and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:09.120
has devised his own carefully crafted method&nbsp;
for delivering the most effective on-command&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:13.560
burps possible. This method also extends&nbsp;
to other bodily noises, such as vomiting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:18.540
as he employs an element of Tuvan throat&nbsp;
singing to produce an especially guttural sound.

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:23.220
LaMarche is actually somewhat famous in the voice&nbsp;
acting world for his fake burping abilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.220 --> 00:21:27.660
and Amy is far from the only character he's&nbsp;
served as the burp double for. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:33.240
he's also provided belches for Wacko on Animaniacs&nbsp;
and Will Ferrell as Buddy the Elf in Elf.

00:21:39.794 --> 00:21:41.294
"Did you hear that?!"

